window.AG_CHAPTERS["18"] = {"id":"18","title":"The Stone in His Chest","titleZh":"胸中的石頭","patient":{"name":"Steward Guo 郭掌櫃","sprite":"man"},"scene":{"img":"art/scene-treatment.jpg","key":"rain"},"pattern":"Heart Blood Stasis 心血瘀阻","principle":"move blood, break stasis, open the chest and free the yang","tongue":"dark purple, with stasis spots at the edges; the vessels under the tongue are dark and full","pulseNote":{"en":"Rough under the finger, like a small knife scraping bamboo — and every so often it misses.","zh":"應指艱澀，如輕刀刮竹，且時而一止"},"needleAsk":{"en":"Needle <b>X&#236;m&#233;n PC4</b>. Tap its exact location.","zh":"行針 — 點出穴位精確位置"},"target":{"id":"PC4","zh":"郄門","py":"X&#236;m&#233;n","x":199,"y":447,"img":"art/body-forearm-palmar.jpg","w":434,"h":1159,"view":{"x":96,"y":320,"w":210,"h":350},"decoys":[{"x":201,"y":570},{"x":140,"y":652},{"x":199,"y":529},{"x":199,"y":365},{"x":238,"y":450}],"note":"<b>郄門 X&#236;m&#233;n PC4</b> — five cun above the wrist crease, on the line joining PC3 and PC7, between the tendons of palmaris longus and flexor carpi radialis. Cleft point of the Pericardium: it invigorates blood, opens the chest and settles the spirit, and is the point reached for in sudden severe chest pain and in palpitations with an irregular beat.<br>腕橫紋上五寸，曲澤與大陵連線上，掌長肌腱與橈側腕屈肌腱之間。心包郄穴。活血、寬胸、寧心。主暴發心胸痛及心悸脈結代。"},"hints":{"ask":{"en":"Ask him to <b>describe</b> the pain, not to rate it. The word he chooses is the finding.","zh":"令其<b>狀</b>其痛，勿問其輕重。所用之字，即為其證。"},"look":{"en":"Look at the colour of the tongue body itself, then lift it and look underneath.","zh":"先觀舌質之色，次舉舌而觀其下。"},"pulse":{"en":"Feel for two things: how the beat <b>travels</b>, and whether it ever fails to arrive.","zh":"當候二事：其來去之<b>滑澀</b>，及其至否有闕。"},"dx":{"en":"Let the quality of the pain name the pathology. Then let the pulse tell you it has been going on a long time.","zh":"由痛之性質以定其病理，由脈以知其病之久。"},"rx":{"en":"The Pericardium protects the Heart, and its cleft point is for the sudden and the severe.","zh":"心包代心受邪，其郄穴主暴急。"},"tech":{"en":"Something is blocking, so there is something to move. But look again at how thin he has become.","zh":"有所阻，則有可通。然再觀其人之瘦削。"}},"reactions":[{"who":"master","en":"Master Shen puts two fingers on the man&#39;s wrist and keeps them there far longer than usual.","zh":"參師父二指按其寸口，久久不去，較常時為長。"},{"who":"patient","en":"Steward Guo sits very still. Sitting still seems to be a skill he has learned.","zh":"郭掌櫃端坐不動。那不動，像是學來的本事。"},{"who":"boy","en":"The herb boy notices the colour of his lips and looks at the floor.","zh":"藥童見其唇色，低頭看地。"},{"who":"master","en":"\"Ask him where it goes. Pain that travels is telling you which road it uses.\"","zh":"「問他痛往何處去。痛能行者，正告你所行何道。」"},{"who":"patient","en":"\"Down the inside of this arm,\" he says, tracing it. \"To the little finger, some nights.\"","zh":"「順著這條手臂內側，」他比劃著，「有些夜裡，一直到小指。」"},{"who":"master","en":"\"Note the hour, too. This one prefers the dark.\"","zh":"「並記其時。此痛喜夜。」"}],"remedy":{"ask":"pain_quality","look":"cold_heat","pulse":"cold_heat","dx":"pain_quality","rx":"channel_organ","tech":"excess_def"},"safety":{"ask":{"en":"Before you name a pattern — is this safe to keep evaluating in an acupuncture setting?","zh":"在辨證之前——這個病人適合繼續在針灸診間評估嗎？"},"lead":{"en":"Crushing chest pain that spreads down the left arm, in a man in his fifties, with an irregular pulse. Screen him before you do anything else.","zh":"五旬男子，胸痛如壓，牽引左臂，脈見結代。在做任何事之前，先做安全篩查。"},"opts":[{"t":"Ask whether the pain comes on with exertion and eases with rest, and whether it has been getting worse or more frequent","c":"問其痛是否因勞而發、得息而緩，近日是否加重或加頻","ok":1,"note":"Correct, and this is the question that changes the day. Chest pain brought on by effort and relieved by rest is the classical description of cardiac ischaemia, and pain that is getting worse, lasting longer, or coming at rest is the pattern that needs emergency assessment, not a treatment room. Steward Guo&#39;s pain is unchanged in six months, comes at night rather than on the stairs, and he has never once had to stop walking because of it.<br><b>Safe to continue — and to be re-screened every visit.</b><br>因勞而發、得息而緩者，正西醫所謂心肌缺血之典型。若其痛日增、時長、或靜而亦發，則當急診，非診間所宜。郭掌櫃之痛半年不變，發於夜而不發於登階，未嘗因痛而輟步。可續，然每診當復篩之。"},{"t":"Ask about cold sweat, nausea, breathlessness at rest, fainting, or pain spreading to the jaw","c":"問冷汗、噁心、靜息氣促、暈厥、痛引下頜","ok":1,"note":"Correct. These travel with an acute cardiac event and any one of them, in a man with chest pain, means stop and send. He has none: no sweating, no nausea, he climbs the granary stairs without stopping, and the pain has never reached his jaw.<br><b>Safe to continue.</b><br>此數者皆急性心疾之伴見。胸痛之人見其一，即當止而轉之。本案皆無：無汗、無噁心、登倉階不喘、痛未及頜。可續。"},{"t":"Ask about swelling of both legs, waking at night short of breath, and needing to sleep propped up","c":"問雙下肢腫、夜間陣發氣促、須高枕而臥","ok":1,"note":"Correct — the screen for a failing heart rather than an obstructed one. He sleeps flat, his ankles are dry, and he wakes from pain rather than from breathlessness.<br><b>Safe to continue.</b><br>此為心衰之篩，非心痹之篩。本案能平臥、踝不腫、夜醒因痛而非因喘。可續。"},{"t":"Skip the screening — the tongue and pulse will tell you everything","c":"不必篩查，舌脈自足以知之","ok":0,"note":"They will not, and this is the one presentation where that shortcut is dangerous. A purple tongue and a choppy pulse are entirely compatible with a heart that is about to fail badly. Pattern differentiation tells you how to treat; screening tells you <i>whether</i> to treat here at all, and it comes first. Chest pain is referred, documented, and re-screened — every visit, without exception. 不然。惟此一證，斷不可省。舌紫脈澀，與心疾將劇者並不相悖。辨證所以定其治，篩查所以定其可治與否，故篩查在先。凡胸痛者，必轉介、必記錄、必每診復篩，無一例外。"}]},"reward":{"cardEn":"Blood That Has Stopped Moving","cardZh":"心血瘀阻 · 通則不痛","cardTxt":"Pain has grammar. Distending pain that wanders is qi; dull pain that likes pressure is emptiness; burning pain is fire; and pain that stabs, stays in one place and refuses to move is blood that has stopped moving. Add the marks of old stasis — a dark purple tongue, full vessels underneath it, dusky lips and nails, a rough pulse that sometimes misses a beat — and the diagnosis is written before you have asked a second question. Where there is free passage there is no pain; where there is pain there is no free passage.","herbId":"honghua","herbEn":"Safflower","herbZh":"紅花","herbImg":"art/card-honghua.jpg","sprite":"art/sprite-renshen-jump.webp","herbTxt":"Acrid and warm, entering Heart and Liver. It invigorates blood and breaks stasis, and it is dose-dependent in a way worth knowing: a small amount nourishes and moves blood, a large amount breaks it. It is contraindicated in pregnancy and used with care in anyone who bleeds easily — a herb that moves blood will move blood you wanted to keep."},"endings":{"three":["A month later Steward Guo says the nights are quiet. He has also, on your advice, seen a physician about the missed beats.<br><b>Master Shen:</b> \"You let the word <i>stabbing</i> do the work, and you did not stop at the blood — you found the emptiness underneath it. And you sent him on. A physician who cannot say <i>this is beyond me</i> is not yet a physician.\"","一月後，郭掌櫃言夜間得安。且依你之言，已就醫察其脈之結代。<br><b>參師父：</b>「你用『刺』字為據，且不止於瘀，更得其下之虛。又能遣之而使就他醫。醫者不能言『此非我所能』，猶未足為醫也。」"],"two":["The nights are better. He still tires climbing the granary stairs.<br><b>Master Shen:</b> \"You moved the blood. Now ask why it stopped moving in a man who was strong at forty.\"","夜間稍安，然登倉階猶乏。<br><b>參師父：</b>「血已行矣。當更問：四十尚健之人，其血何以遂止？」"],"one":["Nothing has changed, and he apologises for taking your time.<br><b>Master Shen:</b> \"He gave you the word in his first breath. <i>Stabbing.</i> Begin there.\"","一如其舊，而反歉佔你之時。<br><b>參師父：</b>「他頭一口氣就把那個字給了你——『刺』。從那裡重來。」"],"titles":["Steward Guo Sleeps Through","Steward Guo Is Easier","Steward Guo Sits Very Still"]},"script":[{"w":"master","en":"Chest pain. Before anything else you will screen him, and if there is any doubt you will send him on. Say it back to me.","cn":"胸痛之證。餘事未論，先做安全篩查；有疑則遣之就醫。你複述一遍。"},{"w":"patient","en":"I keep the salt ledgers. Half a year now, something takes hold in here at night.","cn":"我管鹽行的帳。半年了，一到夜裡，這裡就發作。"},{"w":"patient","en":"Not an ache. Like an awl. Always the same spot — I could put a coin on it.","cn":"不是酸痛。像錐子扎。永遠是同一處——拿個銅錢就能蓋住。"},{"w":"patient","en":"It goes down this arm. And my wife says my lips have gone the colour of a plum.","cn":"順著這條手臂下去。內人說我嘴唇成了梅子的顏色。"},{"w":"master","en":"An awl, one spot, at night, with those lips. Screen him first. Then tell me what kind of pain that is.","cn":"如錐、一處、夜作，而唇色如此。先篩查。而後告我：此屬何等之痛？"}],"qa":[{"q":"Describe the pain in one word.","cq":"以一字狀其痛。","good":1,"a":"Stabbing. Like an awl going in and staying in.","ca":"刺。如錐入而不出。","clue":{"en":"Stabbing pain","zh":"痛如錐刺"},"note":"The quality of pain is a diagnosis, not a detail. <b>Stabbing</b> is blood stasis. <b>Distending</b> is qi stagnation. <b>Dull and improved by pressure</b> is deficiency. <b>Burning</b> is fire. <b>Heavy</b> is damp. <b>Cramping, better with warmth</b> is cold. Learn the six words and half of pain diagnosis is done. 痛之性質即診斷，非細節也。<b>刺痛</b>主血瘀，<b>脹痛</b>主氣滯，<b>隱痛喜按</b>主虛，<b>灼痛</b>主火，<b>重痛</b>主濕，<b>絞痛得溫則減</b>主寒。記此六字，痛證之辨已得其半。"},{"q":"Does the pain move, or stay in one place?","cq":"其痛移否？抑或固定一處？","good":1,"a":"One place. I could cover it with a coin and never miss.","ca":"一處。以銅錢覆之，分毫不差。","clue":{"en":"Fixed location, does not wander","zh":"痛處固定不移"},"note":"Fixed confirms stasis; wandering would have said qi. Blood is substantial, so where it stops, it stops. Qi is not, so its pain drifts, changes with mood, and moves with a sigh. 痛處固定則主瘀，走竄則主氣。血為有形，其停有定所；氣為無形，故其痛游移，隨情志而變，得太息而移。"},{"q":"Is it worse at any particular hour?","cq":"何時為甚？","good":1,"a":"After dark. Always after dark.","ca":"入夜。總是入夜之後。","clue":{"en":"Worse at night","zh":"入夜痛甚"},"note":"Blood belongs to yin, and night is yin — as yang withdraws and blood slows, a stasis that was tolerable by day becomes an awl. Worse at night is a small, reliable marker of blood rather than qi. 血屬陰，夜亦屬陰。陽入而血行漸緩，晝可忍者，夜則如錐。夜甚一證，辨血辨氣之小而可信者。"},{"q":"Let me see the underside of your tongue.","cq":"請舉舌，觀其舌下。","good":1,"a":"He lifts it. The two vessels beneath are dark, thick and standing up.","ca":"舉舌，其下二絡青紫粗脹，怒張而起。","clue":{"en":"Sublingual vessels dark, thick and distended","zh":"舌下絡脈青紫粗脹"},"note":"The most direct window onto blood stasis in the whole examination, and the one students forget to open. Normal sublingual vessels are thin and pale-blue. Dark, thick, tortuous or beaded vessels are stasis you can see, and they often show before the tongue body itself turns purple. 全部診法中，察瘀最直捷者，而學者每忘舉舌。常人舌下絡脈細而淡青；青紫、粗脹、迂曲、成串者，瘀之可見者也，且多先於舌質之變紫。"},{"q":"Have you ever taken a hard blow to the chest, or been ill in bed a long time?","cq":"胸部曾受重擊否？或久病臥床否？","good":1,"a":"A cart shaft caught me here, eleven years ago. I thought nothing of it.","ca":"十一年前，車轅撞在此處。當時不以為意。","clue":{"en":"Old chest trauma","zh":"胸部舊傷"},"note":"Stasis needs a cause, and old trauma is one of the four: injury, long-standing qi stagnation, cold congealing the blood, and qi too weak to push blood along. Asking for the cause is what turns a description into an explanation. 瘀必有因，舊傷其一也。瘀之四因：外傷、氣滯日久、寒凝血澀、氣虛不能行血。求其因者，所以化其狀為其理。"},{"q":"How is your strength these days, compared with ten years ago?","cq":"較十年前，氣力何如？","good":1,"a":"Less. I take the granary stairs slowly now, and I sweat doing it.","ca":"不如前。如今上倉階要慢慢走，走了還出汗。","clue":{"en":"Reduced stamina, sweating on exertion","zh":"動則氣短自汗"},"note":"The finding that saves the treatment. Stasis is the branch; something let it form. Qi too weak to move blood is the commonest reason in a man of this age, and it means you cannot simply break stasis and leave — breaking without supplementing empties him further and the stasis reforms. 此一證所以全其治。瘀為標，必有使之成者。此年之人，多因氣虛不能行血。既知其虛，則不可但破其瘀而已——破而不補，其虛益甚，而瘀復結。"},{"q":"Is the pain relieved by pressing on it?","cq":"按之其痛減否？","good":1,"a":"No — pressing makes it worse. I have to sit still and wait it out.","ca":"不減。按之反甚。惟端坐以待其過。","clue":{"en":"Pain refuses pressure","zh":"痛而拒按"},"note":"Refusing pressure marks an excess, and it is how you keep the branch and the root straight. He is deficient underneath — but the pain itself is a full blockage, and full blockages refuse the hand. Deficient pain welcomes it. 拒按者屬實，所以分標本也。其本雖虛，而痛則為實邪之阻，實則拒按；虛痛則喜按。"}],"look":[{"t":"Dark purple body with stasis spots at the edges","c":"舌質紫暗，邊有瘀點","ok":1,"clue":{"en":"Dark purple tongue with stasis spots","zh":"舌紫暗有瘀點"},"note":"Correct. Colour is the tongue&#39;s slowest-changing property, so a purple body means the stasis is old, not from this week. Spots and patches localise it. Purple with a <i>dark</i> cast means stasis; purple that is bluish and wet leans toward cold congealing the blood; purple that is reddish and dry leans toward heat scorching it. Read the shade, not only the word. 是。舌色變最遲，故舌質紫者，其瘀已久，非旬日之事。瘀點瘀斑則指其所在。紫而<b>晦暗</b>者主瘀；紫而青潤者偏寒凝；紫而絳乾者偏熱灼。當察其色之偏，勿徒執一「紫」字。"},{"t":"Pale and swollen with a white slippery coat","c":"舌淡胖，苔白滑","ok":0,"note":"Yang deficiency with fluid standing. He is deficient, but a pale wet tongue does not explain an awl in one fixed place at night. 陽虛水停之舌。其人誠虛，然舌淡而潤者，不足以解夜作固定之錐痛。"},{"t":"Red with a yellow greasy coat","c":"舌紅苔黃膩","ok":0,"note":"Damp-heat. There is no heat in this case at all — no thirst, no fever, no dark urine, no bitter mouth. 濕熱之舌。本案通身無熱：不渴、不熱、尿不黃、口不苦。"},{"t":"Pale and thin with a thin white coat","c":"舌淡瘦，苔薄白","ok":0,"note":"Blood deficiency, which is close to half the truth — he <i>is</i> depleted. But a blood-deficient tongue is under-filled and pale, not dark; the purple is what separates a shortage of blood from blood that has stopped moving. 血虛之舌，得其半——其人誠虧。然血虛之舌不充而淡，非晦暗。紫者，正所以別「血少」與「血止」也。"}],"pulse":[{"t":"Choppy, with an occasional missed beat 澀而結代","ok":1,"clue":{"en":"Choppy pulse with missed beats","zh":"脈澀而結代"},"note":"Correct, and it is two findings. <b>Choppy</b> — rough, hesitant, like a light blade scraping bamboo — is the pulse of blood that is not flowing smoothly, whether from stasis or from shortage. <b>Knotted or intermittent</b> — a beat that fails to arrive — points to the Heart itself and, in the modern clinic, is one of the findings that earns a referral. Together they say: blood, Heart, and long-standing. 是，而其中有二。<b>澀</b>者，往來艱難，如輕刀刮竹，血行不利之脈，或瘀或虛。<b>結代</b>者，脈來有闕，其病在心，於今日之診間，亦為當轉介之一據。二者合言：病在血、在心，而其來已久。"},{"t":"Wiry and rapid 弦數","ok":0,"note":"Liver fire — chapter sixteen. Nothing here is hot or constrained; the pulse is rough, not taut. 肝火之脈，第十六章是也。本案無熱無鬱，其脈澀而不弦。"},{"t":"Slippery and forceful 滑而有力","ok":0,"note":"The opposite quality. Slippery rolls smoothly under the finger like pearls; choppy catches. Learning to feel the difference between slippery and choppy is learning to feel whether blood moves. 其象適反。滑者往來流利如珠走盤，澀者艱澀而滯。能辨滑澀，即能候血之行否。"},{"t":"Floating and tight 浮緊","ok":0,"note":"Wind-cold on the surface — a new illness, three days old. His is half a year old and entirely interior. 表有風寒，新病三日之脈。本案半年，純然在裡。"}],"dx":[{"ask":"Step 1 — the pain has a quality. What pathology does that quality name?<br><small class=\"zh\" style=\"color:var(--ink-soft)\">第一步：痛有其性。此性所主，為何病理？</small>","opts":[{"t":"Stabbing, fixed and worse at night — blood stasis","c":"刺痛、固定、夜甚 — 血瘀","ok":1,"note":"Correct, and three independent markers agree. <b>Stabbing</b> is the classic word for stasis. <b>Fixed</b> confirms it, because substance stays where it stops. <b>Worse at night</b> fits, because blood is yin and slows as yang withdraws. Any one of the three could be argued with; all three together, with a purple tongue and a choppy pulse, is not a case that needs a fourth opinion. 是，三證獨立而同歸。<b>刺痛</b>者，瘀之定名。<b>固定</b>者，有形之邪，其停有所。<b>夜甚</b>者，血屬陰，陽入而其行愈緩。三者有一猶可議，三者俱見而兼舌紫脈澀，則不待更求他證。"},{"t":"Distending and wandering — qi stagnation","c":"脹痛走竄 — 氣滯","ok":0,"note":"That is the pattern next door, and telling them apart is the point of the question. Qi pain distends rather than stabs, wanders rather than fixes, changes with mood, and eases with a sigh or a belch. His does none of that. And note the order in the clinic: qi moves blood, so qi stagnation left long enough <i>becomes</i> blood stasis — his may well have started there eleven years ago. 此其鄰證，能辨之乃本問之意。氣痛脹而不刺，走竄而不定，隨情志而變，得太息噯氣則減。本案皆不然。然當知其序：氣為血帥，氣滯日久<b>則成</b>血瘀。十一年前，其始或正在此。"},{"t":"Dull and relieved by pressure — deficiency","c":"隱痛喜按 — 虛","ok":0,"note":"He is deficient, and that matters — but not here. Deficient pain is dull, comes on when he is tired, and welcomes a warm hand. His refuses the hand outright. Root and branch are different questions; this one is about the branch. 其人誠虛，且不可忽——然不在此問。虛痛隱隱，遇勞而發，喜溫喜按。本案拒按甚明。標本異問，此問所辨者標也。"},{"t":"Burning — fire","c":"灼痛 — 火","ok":0,"note":"Fire pain burns, comes with thirst, a red tongue and a rapid pulse, and is usually worse in the day. He has a purple tongue, no thirst and a choppy pulse. 火痛為灼，兼渴、舌紅、脈數，且多晝甚。本案舌紫、不渴、脈澀。"}]},{"ask":"Step 2 — the pulse misses beats. What does that add?<br><small class=\"zh\" style=\"color:var(--ink-soft)\">第二步：脈來有闕。此又何所增益？</small>","opts":[{"t":"It names the organ, and it is why he must also be seen by a physician","c":"定其臟，且為必當轉介之由","ok":1,"note":"Correct on both counts. A knotted or intermittent pulse locates the trouble at the Heart rather than in the chest wall or the channels, which is what lifts this from a musculoskeletal case to a Heart case. And in modern practice an irregular beat with chest pain is a finding you document and refer, every time — treating the pattern and referring the patient are not alternatives, they happen together. 二者皆是。結代之脈，定其病在心，而不在胸壁經絡，遂由痹痛之屬而入心病之屬。且於今之臨證，胸痛而脈律不齊者，必記錄、必轉介，無有例外。辨證施治與轉介，非二者擇一，乃並行之事。"},{"t":"It means the stasis is severe and nothing more","c":"惟言其瘀之甚，餘無所指","ok":0,"note":"It does suggest severity, but stopping there is what makes the error dangerous. A missed beat is information about the organ, and about whether this room is the right room. 其言瘀甚誠有之，然止於此則其誤可危。脈有闕者，所報在臟，且在「此室是否其所」。"},{"t":"Missed beats are common and can be ignored in a calm patient","c":"脈有闕者常見，其人安靜則可不論","ok":0,"note":"Not with chest pain. In another presentation an occasional irregularity may be unremarkable; with fixed stabbing chest pain radiating down the arm it is part of a picture that needs a physician&#39;s assessment alongside yours. 胸痛者不然。他證見之或無足論；若胸痛如刺、固定、放射至臂，則其脈之闕為此圖之一部，當與醫者共察之。"},{"t":"It means the patient is anxious","c":"其人多慮所致","ok":0,"note":"Anxiety can quicken a pulse. It does not delete beats. Do not explain away a physical finding with a psychological one — that is the reasoning that gets a patient into trouble. 慮可令脈數，不能令脈闕。勿以情志之說而消形質之證；此等推理，最易誤人。"}]},{"ask":"Step 3 — what is the root, and what is the branch?<br><small class=\"zh\" style=\"color:var(--ink-soft)\">第三步：孰為其本？孰為其標？</small>","opts":[{"t":"Root: qi too weak to move blood. Branch: the stasis it allowed to form","c":"本：氣虛不能行血。標：因而所結之瘀","ok":1,"note":"Correct, and it decides the whole treatment. Qi is the commander of blood — blood does not move itself. A man who now climbs stairs slowly and sweats doing it has lost the force that pushes blood along, and where blood slows, it congeals. So this is deficiency at the root with excess at the branch. Break the stasis and support the qi together: break without supporting and you empty him further, and the stasis simply reforms; support without breaking and you are feeding a blocked road. 是，一言而定全治。氣為血之帥，血不自行。今登階而遲、動則自汗，是推血之力已減；血行既緩，遂凝而為瘀。故其證本虛而標實。當破瘀與益氣並行：但破不補，則其虛益甚而瘀復結；但補不破，則所養者塞途之血耳。"},{"t":"Pure excess — there is a blockage, so remove it","c":"純實 — 有所阻，去之而已","ok":0,"note":"The pain refuses pressure, so the branch is certainly full. But look at the man: less strength than ten years ago, breathless on the granary stairs, sweating with effort. Treat him as purely full and he will feel better for a fortnight and worse by the next season. 痛而拒按，其標誠實。然觀其人：氣力不如十年前，登倉階則喘，動則自汗。純以實治之，則旬日稍安，逾季而愈劇。"},{"t":"Pure deficiency — supplement and the pain will go","c":"純虛 — 但補則痛自止","ok":0,"note":"Supplementing into a blocked vessel is the classic way to make stasis pain worse. There is something substantial in the way; it has to be moved as well as fed. 塞而補之，瘀痛之所以益甚也。其中有形之邪，當行之而後養之。"},{"t":"Root: cold congealing the blood","c":"本：寒凝血澀","ok":0,"note":"A real cause of stasis, and always worth testing. But cold-congealed pain eases with warmth, comes with cold limbs, a pale wet tongue and a slow pulse. His pain is unchanged by warmth and his tongue is dark rather than pale. 寒凝誠為瘀之一因，每當試之。然寒凝之痛得溫則減，兼肢冷、舌淡潤、脈遲。本案得溫不減，舌晦而不淡。"}]}],"rx":[{"id":"PC4","t":"X&#236;m&#233;n PC4 郄門","ok":1,"note":"Cleft point of the Pericardium — and you have met the rule once already, in chapter sixteen: cleft points treat the acute and the painful, and on the yin channels they also treat blood. LU6 used the blood half of that rule for a Lung that was bleeding; PC4 uses the pain half for a Heart that is blocked. One rule, two chapters, two faces. 心包之郄穴。此例第十六章已一見：郄穴主急症痛症，陰經郄穴兼主血。孔最用其「血」之半，以肺絡出血；郄門用其「痛」之半，以心脈痹阻。一例二章，二面而已。"},{"id":"PC6","t":"N&#232;igu&#257;n PC6 內關","ok":1,"note":"Connecting point of the Pericardium and confluent point of the Yin Wei vessel — the point that opens the chest, regulates Heart qi and settles palpitations. In this case it is the workhorse and PC4 is the specialist: PC6 for the ongoing chest and the rhythm, PC4 for the pain when it seizes. 心包絡穴，八脈交會通陰維。寬胸、理心氣、定悸。於本案，內關為常用之主，郄門為應急之專：內關治其胸與其律，郄門治其發時之痛。"},{"id":"ST36","t":"Z&#250;s&#257;nl&#464; ST36 足三里","ok":1,"note":"Here it treats the root. Qi is the commander of blood, and this stasis formed because the commander weakened — so building qi at the great tonifying point is not a detour, it is the half of the treatment that keeps the stasis from reforming after you break it. 於此所治者本也。氣為血帥，其瘀之成，以帥弱故。取補氣之要穴，非迂道也，乃破瘀之後、使不復結之半。"},{"id":"LU6","t":"K&#466;ngzu&#236; LU6 孔最","ok":0,"note":"The right rule and the wrong channel. LU6 is the Lung&#39;s cleft point, for a Lung that is bleeding or in acute spasm. His trouble is the Heart, and there is no bleeding. 例是而經非。孔最為肺之郄，主肺絡出血及急喘。本案病在心，且無出血。"},{"id":"KI7","t":"F&#249;li&#363; KI7 復溜","ok":0,"note":"Warms Kidney yang and moves standing water. He has no oedema, no cold, no daybreak diarrhoea — none of the picture that point answers. 溫腎陽而行停水。本案無腫、無寒、無五更瀉，其穴所應者，一無所見。"},{"id":"LR3","t":"T&#224;ich&#333;ng LR3 太衝","ok":0,"note":"Defensible in a stasis case, since the Liver stores blood and moving Liver qi does move blood — but here the trouble is fixed in the chest, not constrained and wandering, and the deficiency underneath means a moving-and-draining point is not where you start. 於瘀證取之，其說可通：肝藏血，疏肝氣則血亦行。然本案痛固定在胸，非鬱而走竄；且其下有虛，疏泄之穴非所先。"}],"tech":[{"t":"Even method — move the blood at the local points, reinforce at the tonifying points","c":"平補平瀉 — 局部行血，補穴用補","ok":1,"note":"<b>Correct, and this is what a root-deficient, branch-excess case actually requires.</b> One technique for the whole treatment would be wrong in one direction or the other: drain everywhere and you empty a man who is already short; reinforce everywhere and you feed a blockage. So the technique follows the point&#39;s job — move where there is stasis, build where there is shortage. Mixed patterns take mixed technique. 是。本虛標實之證，正當如此。若通身一法，非失之瀉即失之補：概瀉則奪其已虧，概補則養其所塞。故手法從穴之所司：瘀處行之，虛處建之。虛實夾雜者，其法亦夾。"},{"t":"Strong reducing throughout, to break the stasis","c":"通身重瀉，以破其瘀","ok":0,"note":"It would move the blood, and it would cost more than it moved. Read him again: slower on the stairs, sweating with effort, thinner than he was. Vigorous draining in a depleted patient buys a good fortnight and a worse season. 血誠可行，然所費過於所得。再觀其人：登階而遲、動則汗、形較昔瘦。虛人而峻瀉之，得旬日之安，而後一季更劇。"},{"t":"Reinforcing throughout, since the root is deficiency","c":"通身用補，以其本虛","ok":0,"note":"Half the case, treated as if it were the whole case. The pain refuses pressure — there is something substantial in the way, and supplementing into an obstruction is how stasis pain is made worse. 得其半而以為全。痛而拒按，其中有形之邪；塞而補之，瘀痛所以益甚。"},{"t":"Reinforcing with generous moxa","c":"補法，重灸","ok":0,"note":"Moxa does move blood and would not be absurd — but he has no cold signs at all, and warming a patient whose pulse is already irregular, without addressing the blockage, treats the wrong half. Warmth for cold-congealed stasis; here the stasis is from weakness, not from cold. 灸能行血，其說非謬。然本案通身無寒之候；脈律已不齊而但溫之、不通其阻，是治其非所急者。寒凝之瘀當溫；本案之瘀由虛而非由寒。"}]};
